    'option_d' => 'No fee may ever be charged for producing records',
    'correct_option' => 'c',
    'explanation' => 'A. Incorrect. A practice policy cannot override applicable access and fee laws. A fee is not automatically lawful merely because it appears in informed-consent paperwork.

B. Incorrect. Record-access fees cannot be used as a collection penalty. An unpaid treatment balance must be addressed separately.

C. Correct. A provider may generally charge a reasonable, cost-based fee for producing copies or an agreed-upon summary. Permitted costs may include copying labor, supplies, postage, and requested portable electronic media, subject to applicable limits. The fee should reflect the actual production process rather than discourage the client from exercising access rights.

D. Incorrect. Some qualifying requests may receive special no-cost treatment, but record copies are not universally required to be free.

CTA Exam Strategy: Separate:Reasonable production feefromPunitive charge or collection leverage',
